content.text:
It uses `s.loadSessionState`, `s.clearSessionState`, `s.CondenseSession`, `s.saveSessionState`, `s.cleanupShadowBranchIfUnused` — so it's deeply tied to the receiver's private methods. Can't easily make it a package-level function.

But zooming out — `runAttach` also uses `strat` for `InitializeSession` and `SaveStep`, which are on the `Strategy` interface. The only reason it needs the concrete type is `CondenseSessionByID`. 

The simplest fix: `GetStrategy` already returns `*ManualCommitStrategy`, so `condenseAndFinalizeSession` doesn't need `strat` passed in at all — it can just call `GetStrategy(ctx)` itself. That removes the parameter and makes the coupling no worse than it already is at the `GetStrategy` level.

```go
func condenseAndFinalizeSession(ctx context.Context, sessionID string) (string, error) {
    strat := GetStrategy(ctx)
    // ... rest unchanged
}
```

Then in `runAttach`, the single `strat := GetStrategy(ctx)` call is only used for `InitializeSession` and `SaveStep` (both on the interface), and `condenseAndFinalizeSession` is self-contained. Doesn't fix the deep coupling, but removes the need to thread `strat` through and makes the function signature cleaner.

